1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,403
We built ProofGrid to turn solar reports into evidence reviewers can trace and test.

2
00:00:06,403 --> 00:00:15,549
The working application registered a published historical solar reference: twenty four hours totaling forty point seven one nine kilowatt hours.

3
00:00:15,549 --> 00:00:21,952
It then rejected all twenty four repeated meter hours, even after the readings changed.

4
00:00:21,952 --> 00:00:34,758
For the Renewable Energy and Energy Trading track, we connected data quality, atomic registration, and verifiable exports in one workflow for small solar operators and community energy reviewers.

5
00:00:34,758 --> 00:00:42,792
We brought the energy profile, candidate total, raw readings, and quality findings into a single review workspace.

6
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:51,771
The input format uses a meter identifier, a supplied capacity bound, and hourly energy values in a simple CSV.

7
00:00:51,771 --> 00:00:54,606
Each timestamp identifies one UTC hour.

8
00:00:54,606 --> 00:01:03,585
Reviewers can connect a chart peak to its underlying reading and check the relevant finding in the same interface.

9
00:01:03,585 --> 00:01:08,784
Synthetic examples and published historical data demonstrate this consistent review process.

10
00:01:08,784 --> 00:01:14,588
We implemented explainable checks that turn data problems into specific findings.

11
00:01:14,588 --> 00:01:22,502
The faulty synthetic sample produces three blocking issues, and the batch cannot proceed to registration.

12
00:01:22,502 --> 00:01:29,888
Capacity, repeated timestamps, continuity, formatting, and nonnegative energy checks establish a clear input contract.

13
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:34,637
A separate peak comparison flags values for reviewer attention.

14
00:01:34,637 --> 00:01:44,134
This distinction keeps blocking rules explicit while supporting informed human judgement on readings that deserve a closer look.

15
00:01:44,134 --> 00:01:52,089
We transformed published Open Power System Data and CoSSMic cumulative readings into a traceable hourly solar reference.

16
00:01:52,089 --> 00:02:03,321
Its twenty four nominal intervals total forty point seven one nine kilowatt hours, with a peak of seven point nine eight one kilowatt hours.

17
00:02:03,321 --> 00:02:08,936
The repository preserves the selected counters, transformation code, attribution, and file hashes.

18
00:02:08,936 --> 00:02:16,424
The interface links the resulting readings to their published source and configured ten kilowatt review setting.

19
00:02:16,424 --> 00:02:18,764
Regeneration produced identical data hashes.

20
00:02:18,764 --> 00:02:26,313
We connected the review decision to an actual Solidity transaction on a local Ethereum Virtual Machine.

21
00:02:26,313 --> 00:02:33,862
The contract records the evidence digest and reserves the meter hour slots in one atomic operation.

22
00:02:33,862 --> 00:02:38,109
The demonstrated batch receives a successful block two receipt.

23
00:02:38,109 --> 00:02:45,658
Source classification, readings, and verification specifications are committed together, linking the reviewed content to its transaction.

24
00:02:45,658 --> 00:02:51,792
This creates an inspectable registration record that reviewers can export and check again.

25
00:02:51,792 --> 00:02:59,986
We validated duplicate protection by changing the supplied readings and submitting the same twenty four meter hours again.

26
00:02:59,986 --> 00:03:04,993
All twenty four occupied slots were detected, and registration was blocked.

27
00:03:04,993 --> 00:03:11,366
The changed file could not register those hours a second time in this registry.

28
00:03:11,366 --> 00:03:21,382
At the contract level, an automated test submits an overlapping transaction, confirms a reverted receipt, and verifies that no partial registration remains.

29
00:03:21,382 --> 00:03:25,934
The enforcement holds across both the interface and contract execution.

30
00:03:25,934 --> 00:03:33,456
We delivered a portable JSON evidence package and verified the original against four checks.

31
00:03:33,456 --> 00:03:39,366
After one reading was altered, the content check identified the mismatch.

32
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:46,888
Verification combines a recomputed digest, registry identity, on-chain registration, and the successful receipt event.

33
00:03:46,888 --> 00:03:54,410
The interface makes each result visible, connecting the exported content to its registration evidence.

34
00:03:54,410 --> 00:04:00,320
A separate command-line verifier reproduces the content-integrity check outside the application.

35
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:08,460
ProofGrid delivers an implemented, tested path from solar readings to registered and verifiable evidence.

36
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:16,018
Eleven automated tests pass across validation, contract execution, source classification, and evidence integrity.

37
00:04:16,018 --> 00:04:25,321
The demonstrated workflow records twenty four meter hours, blocks repeated registration, and detects an edited export.

38
00:04:25,321 --> 00:04:29,391
Four verification checks make the result inspectable.

39
00:04:29,391 --> 00:04:36,368
Published code, traceable source data, and documented commands make these results reproducible.

40
00:04:36,368 --> 00:04:37,531
Review completed.

41
00:04:37,531 --> 00:04:38,693
Evidence registered.

42
00:04:38,693 --> 00:04:39,856
Verification delivered.

43
00:04:39,856 --> 00:04:41,019
One hour.

44
00:04:41,019 --> 00:04:42,182
One record.
